<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emain Macha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Navan Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emain Macha, now Navan Fort near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> in Northern Ireland, was the legendary royal centre of the Ulaid and the seat of King Conchobar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulster-cycle">Ulster Cycle</a>. Excavations revealed a huge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> building of about 95 BCE, filled with stones and burned. Tradition says it was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-collas">Three Collas</a>, ancestors of the Airgíalla.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emain-macha.jpg" alt="Emain Macha" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrick Brown · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navan_Fort,_County_Armagh_-_geograph.org.uk_-_43871.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
